-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index: each kiosk holds its own on-disk index file, built fresh from the active snapshot. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection — `findBySerial` and its result types are identical regardless of backend.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the new SQLite index into a temporary location from the incoming snapshot; validation and index construction never touch the active index. Activate the new index only after signature verification, schema check, uniqueness validation, and index construction all succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot's index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active SQLite index by the normalized serial number. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt on-kiosk from each signed snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory — one centrally maintained index with smaller kiosk updates, but lookups depend on the lab network and need service-timeout handling; Managed search service — less index maintenance and room for richer search later, but recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity | Kiosks have no network route during normal operation (HLD tenet); the embedded index is the only candidate that keeps every lookup on local files. The other two make lookups depend on the lab network or an external service. The cost lands entirely in this node: each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on every snapshot import. The certificate interface (`findBySerial`) is identical under all three, so this choice is invisible to the certificates node. |
